\chapter{Extension activation and plugin isolation}
\label{ch:112}
\index{Extension activation and plugin isolation}
\index{SDK!dynamic isolation}

Explicit SDK analyzer or emitter modes can discover external providers through \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_PLUGIN\_PATH} or select a provider with \OnxCodeTerm{--plugin +NAME\_OR\_PATH}. Ordinary compilation does not activate those hooks. In the supported Linux x86-64 dynamic profile, a worker enters the configured isolation before loading plugin code. Failed isolation prevents loading. An explicitly configured linked analyzer runs inside its trusted compiler host and may receive the specified structured verified MIR view.

\section{Select authority along with the hook}
Check that the requested input schema and artifact kind match the provider. Exercise one valid selection and one ambiguous or mismatched selection. For a dynamic test, verify failure when the worker cannot establish isolation. For a trusted provider, treat the selected source and its dependencies as part of the host build. Give tests an observer outside the provider so a success message from the plugin is never the sole evidence of artifact publication.

\section{Follow a linked analyzer's diagnostic}
The linked analyzer fixture expects a function called main returning
nineteen times three. On the documented fixture build, select
\OnxCodeTerm{linked-v2:inspect} in SDK analysis mode. Its callback checks
the snapshot has a nonzero revision and verified MIR identity, then
walks instructions looking for exactly one multiply and a constant
nineteen. This is an observation about the supplied representation:
printing 57 from an independently compiled application would not
establish what instructions the analyzer saw.

The callback submits a note from a local character array and immediately
overwrites that array. A correct published note still describes the
multiply. If it contained the replacement characters, the sink would
have retained a pointer to temporary plugin storage instead of copying
the message. In another fixture mode the plugin submits a valid note
followed by invalid UTF-8 and ignores the second submission's failure.
The host discards the staged notes. Contrast an accepted diagnostic of
error severity: it can be published and cause exit status one. A bad
record and a well-formed report of a problem thus require different
observations, even if both commands fail.

Do not put the linked fixture on the dynamic plugin search path to
repeat this experiment. The linked selector uses its configured
provider and cannot be combined with the dynamic selection flag.
The native worker's read-only, digest-checked snapshot mapping belongs
to a different transport. Its documented System V profile does not
provide kernel sealing; a successful digest check is not evidence of
that extra mechanism.

